00:20:48,190 --> 00:21:05,510
to 3.14. And if I keep going, well, nothing much happens until I hit 6 and then the program crashes.

212
00:21:05,510 --> 00:21:11,490
So something interesting is going on here, at least something quirky is going on.

213
00:21:11,490 --> 00:21:18,870
And the reason is, again, it has to do with how data is laid out in memory and how it's accessed.

214
00:21:18,870 --> 00:21:24,510
And one of the features of C and C++ is it doesn't do any bounds checking on arrays.

215
00:21:24,510 --> 00:21:31,890
It will happily let you reference element number of 5 million of a two element array and

216
00:21:31,890 --> 00:21:35,230
not complain, but the operating system might complain.

217
00:21:35,230 --> 00:21:36,230
as it did here.

218
00:21:36,230 --> 00:21:45,200
And in this particular structure, and we'll see more about how structures are implemented and laid out,

219
00:21:45,200 --> 00:21:52,540
but basically the two, if each of these blocks in this vertical chain represents 4 bytes.

220
00:21:52,540 --> 00:21:56,480
And so the two elements of A, each are 4 bytes.

221
00:21:56,480 --> 00:22:05,070
D is 8 bytes, and then there's some other stuff in the other beyond there that that's not actually

222
00:22:05,070 --> 00:22:12,070
in the structure itself. So you'll see that if I reference either A of 0 or A of 1,

223
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:20,180
then I will just modify that array as designed. But when I'm calling fun of 2 or fun of 3,

224
00:22:20,180 --> 00:22:27,560
what I'm actually doing is altering the bytes that encode this number D. And that's why you saw

225
00:22:27,560 --> 00:22:33,960
the sort of funny numbers come out of it. And as I go up, at some point, when I hit 6, I'm modifying

226
00:22:33,960 --> 00:22:40,000
some state of the program that it's using to kind of keep things organized, most likely

227
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:47,780
how it keeps track of allocated memory, and that's causing the program to crash.

228
00:22:47,780 --> 00:22:55,470
So this is a pretty good demonstration of why C programming can drive you crazy. Because, as you

229
00:22:55,470 --> 00:23:01,970
saw it, it doesn't do balance checking. So it's easy to write code that does invalid stuff.

230
00:23:01,970 --> 00:23:11,320
it's also often the case that you'll cause some problem and it has this sort of action-of-distance

231
00:23:11,320 --> 00:23:17,780
feature that you can modify some, think you're modifying some data structure, and what you're doing

232
00:23:17,780 --> 00:23:22,280
because of the way things are organized in memory, you're changing something totally unrelated,

233
00:23:22,480 --> 00:23:23,740
somewhere else in the program.

234
00:23:24,640 --> 00:23:27,400
I mean, imagine they're not just one apart, but they're 10,000 apart.

235
00:23:28,450 --> 00:23:32,530
And things might just run fine for hours, days,

236
00:23:32,550 --> 00:23:39,550
or weeks and then always say at some point that data that got corrupted a long time ago

237
00:23:39,550 --> 00:23:47,350
gets accessed and something goes wrong. So this can be some of the worst debugging nightmares that

238
00:23:47,350 --> 00:23:57,000
exist on Earth is to try and figure out memory referencing errors. So this is actually one

239
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:03,120
argument not to program in C or C++ plus plus, and it's a valid argument, I'll admit. But

240
00:24:03,320 --> 00:24:13,260
Also, as a person who's writing a lot of C programming, you just get more experienced and you know at times that you should actually put bounds checking in your own code.

241
00:24:13,720 --> 00:24:23,470
And there's also tools available that will help you sort of bulletproof your code so that it will detect these kind of problems.

242
00:24:24,090 --> 00:24:28,770
So it's not like you have to change languages, but it is a particular feature of these languages.

243
00:24:30,650 --> 00:24:34,510
And so understanding sort of the machine level representation of data structure of data structure.

244
00:24:34,530 --> 00:24:42,530
and how they work really makes a huge difference in your ability to deal with these kind of vulnerabilities and things.

245
00:24:42,530 --> 00:24:46,530
Vulnerabilities, by the way, also from a security perspective.

246
00:24:46,530 --> 00:24:55,040
The fourth sort of theme we'll cover in the course is getting performance out of programs.

247
00:24:55,040 --> 00:25:03,040
Other parts of the curriculum in CS do much more emphasis on getting the right algorithm or the right data structure.

248
00:25:03,040 --> 00:25:05,040
And that's really well and good.

249
00:25:05,040 --> 00:25:07,040
important stuff, I don't deny it.

250
00:25:07,040 --> 00:25:14,040
But there's some amount of the sort of low-level optimization that you need to do that you need to do,

251
00:25:14,040 --> 00:25:20,040
that you need to understand what the system does, what makes it run well, what makes it run poorly,

252
00:25:20,040 --> 00:25:23,040
in order to be able to do that kind of optimization.

253
00:25:23,040 --> 00:25:29,740
So the example we like to use is these two functions do exactly the same thing in terms

254
00:25:29,740 --> 00:25:33,740
of their behavior.

255
00:25:33,740 --> 00:25:45,740
What they do is copy a matrix or an array from called source or SRC to a destination DST.

256
00:25:45,740 --> 00:25:53,740
They're both sized to be 2,048 rows, 2048 columns, two dimensional arrays.

257
00:25:53,740 --> 00:25:56,740
And you'll see that the programs do the obvious thing.

258
00:25:56,740 --> 00:26:03,520
You have a nested pair of loops to do the row and column indices, and you just copy from one

259
00:26:03,520 --> 00:26:06,520
source element to a destination element.

260
00:26:06,520 --> 00:26:09,520
The only thing that's different you'll see is that

261
00:26:09,520 --> 00:26:13,520
the two loops, their nesting is different.

262
00:26:13,520 --> 00:26:15,520
Their nesting order is different.

263
00:26:15,520 --> 00:26:19,520
In one case, I'm going kind of row first, going through all the rows,

264
00:26:19,520 --> 00:26:24,900
going through all the rows, and then the columns, and the other is

265
00:26:24,900 --> 00:26:26,900
for any given row, I'm copying all the columns.

266
00:26:26,900 --> 00:26:29,900
That's really the only difference between these two programs.

267
00:26:29,900 --> 00:26:33,410
But what you'll find if you run it on a typical

268
00:26:33,410 --> 00:26:37,470
is that one is much faster than the other.

269
00:26:37,470 --> 00:26:43,470
And this particular machine we ran it on, it was about close to 20 times difference in performance.

270
00:26:43,470 --> 00:26:52,470
So something fishy is going on if the same program that differs only in this seemingly insignificant way,

271
00:26:52,470 --> 00:26:58,470
a way that has no effect whatsoever on its functionality, can have this much performance difference.

272
00:26:58,470 --> 00:27:02,630
And so to understand this, you need to stare at the cover of the book.

273
00:27:02,630 --> 00:27:14,120
of the book. Because basically, you're at two different points of this strange-looking

274
00:27:14,120 --> 00:27:20,120
picture that's on your book, and since there's no axes or labels on it, it makes no sense whatsoever.

275
00:27:20,120 --> 00:27:22,150
But it's there.

276
00:27:22,150 --> 00:27:29,150
So what you see is this picture shows for different memory access patterns, and I won't go into the details,

277
00:27:29,150 --> 00:27:36,150
what the throughput measured in megabytes per second on a basic

278
00:27:36,150 --> 00:27:39,150
a copying program was.

279
00:27:39,150 --> 00:27:46,150
And without going into the details, what you'll see is these two functions sort of sit at different points in this memory access pattern.

280
00:27:46,150 --> 00:27:52,150
The one that goes through row by row is much better than the one that goes through column by row.

281
00:27:52,150 --> 00:27:59,050
And as a result, you're getting a lot better performance, and it has to do with this memory hierarchy

282
00:27:59,050 --> 00:28:06,050
and what they call the cache memories, that you're getting way better performance out of it in one case than the other.

283
00:28:06,050 --> 00:28:14,050
So that explains what the cover of the book is about and we'll talk about it more later in the course.

